<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Changsha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Changsha is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> province in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the Xiang River. It was the capital of the Han-era Kingdom of Changsha, whose Mawangdui tombs yielded rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> texts and paintings, and of the Ma family's Chu state in the 10th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> studied and began his political work there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/changsha.jpg" alt="Changsha" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Junchong · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%88%B1%E6%99%9A%E4%BA%AD%EF%BC%88%E7%A7%8B-%E4%BE%A7%E9%9D%A2%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion was rich in shamanistic elements: spirit mediums communicated with deities of rivers, mountains and the sky, as reflected in the Nine Songs of the Songs of Chu, and the Chu Silk Manuscript from Zidanku in <b>Changsha</b> describes cosmology and the spirits of the months.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ceramic technology reached a classical maturity under the Tang, with the white Xing wares, the three-colour (sancai) glazed wares and the painted wares of <b>Changsha</b>, which reached markets across the Islamic world and East Africa and established Chinese ceramics as a prestige commodity in world trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Chinese Civil War, at the time of the peaceful takeover of Hunan in 1949, Song Yang and another musician, Yi Yang, arranged the song for four-part harmony in <b>Changsha</b>; by then it was already widely sung.</blockquote><a href="/songs/red-sun-in-the-sky">Red Sun in the Sky</a></li><li><blockquote>Wu Rui supported Liu Bang against Xiang Yu, and after the Han victory in 202 BCE he was moved to become King of <b>Changsha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hengshan">Kingdom of Hengshan</a></li><li><blockquote>Ma Yin, a carpenter turned soldier from Xuzhou, came to Hunan with a band of mercenaries and made himself master of the region from <b>Changsha</b> in 896.</blockquote><a href="/state/ma-chu">Ma Chu</a></li></ul></section>
